WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
A useful way to discover state is to interrupt a
conversation halfway through it and move

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.950
the next message to a fresh worker. Consider an
email submission dialogue: earlier commands

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:14.648
establish the sender and recipients, while a
later command supplies the message body.

00:00:14.648 --> 00:00:20.090
A worker that sees only the final body cannot
reconstruct the complete operation. It needs

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.592
information retained from the earlier messages.
The diagram shows these pieces accumulating

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:31.034
in one conversation context. State is therefore
not simply data in a database; it includes

00:00:31.034 --> 00:00:36.597
any remembered information that later processing
depends on, including process variables, an

00:00:36.597 --> 00:00:40.769
open connection's context, or a partially
completed user interaction.

00:00:40.769 --> 00:00:46.034
HTTP requests are designed to carry the
information needed to interpret each individual

00:00:46.034 --> 00:00:51.480
exchange, but an HTTP application can still
introduce a dependency on a particular worker.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.534
Imagine worker A storing an unfinished game only
in its own memory.

00:00:55.769 --> 00:01:00.939
The next request contains the game identifier
but reaches worker B, whose dictionary has no

00:01:00.939 --> 00:01:06.166
corresponding entry. Identical code does not
make these workers interchangeable because they

00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:11.336
have different histories. The illustration
follows the identifier to the wrong memory store

00:01:11.336 --> 00:01:13.381
and exposes the missing information.

00:01:13.381 --> 00:01:18.180
Before adding replicas, ask whether another
worker can complete the request using the

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.093
request itself and explicitly shared services,
without consulting the original worker's

00:01:23.093 --> 00:01:23.997
private history.

00:01:23.997 --> 00:01:29.214
Start with a chess application that keeps a
dictionary of active games inside one process.

00:01:29.214 --> 00:01:34.489
Cloning that process creates several independent
dictionaries, so each game becomes tied to

00:01:34.489 --> 00:01:39.533
the worker holding it. Instead, put the
authoritative game record in shared storage and

00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:41.794
include its identifier in each request.

00:01:41.997 --> 00:01:47.510
A worker loads the board, validates the
requested move, saves the result, and returns
the

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:52.962
updated representation. A replacement worker can
now continue after the original process

00:01:52.962 --> 00:01:58.662
disappears. The diagram shows two
interchangeable application workers converging
on one game

00:01:58.662 --> 00:01:59.095
record.

00:01:59.095 --> 00:02:04.137
We have removed a placement constraint from the
application tier, but we have deliberately

00:02:04.137 --> 00:02:08.618
created a shared dependency that needs its own
capacity and availability design.

00:02:08.618 --> 00:02:13.605
Externalizing state does not automatically make
concurrent updates correct. Suppose two

00:02:13.605 --> 00:02:18.763
requests both read game version twelve. The
first valid move updates the record to version

00:02:18.763 --> 00:02:23.922
thirteen. If the second request blindly writes
its earlier view, it can erase the accepted

00:02:23.922 --> 00:02:26.444
change or apply a move to an obsolete board.

00:02:26.618 --> 00:02:31.547
A conditional update that succeeds only while
the version is still twelve lets exactly one

00:02:31.547 --> 00:02:36.422
of these competing updates win. The second
request must reload and reconsider its action.

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:41.352
The diagram marks the two reads and the rejected
stale write; it makes clear that a common

00:02:41.352 --> 00:02:45.295
storage location and a correct update protocol
solve different problems.
